    835:| §C9.a NOVELTY_CHALLENGES — Divisor-only restriction at primorial Q: does `M_W^{div}(n) := Σ_{q | W, q sqf} mu(gcd(q,n)) / phi(q/gcd(q,n))` admit an exact pointwise closed form? (cross-domain: NONE; project-internal Möbius collapse + Hölder-reduced Ramanujan sum) | BUILT | E | S208 novelty-mode session refining E2.1 / S191 / C9. **Theorem (S208, proven analytically; verified pointwise to machine epsilon at N ≤ 10⁶ across 10 conductors).** For every squarefree integer W ≥ 1 and every positive integer n, `M_W^{div}(n) = [gcd(n, W) = 1] · W / phi(W)`. For non-squarefree W, the identity holds with W replaced by rad(W). **Proof**: factorise the squarefree-divisor lattice of W by intersection with `{p|W : p|n}`; the alternating-sum collapse `Σ_{A⊆P_n}(-1)^|A| = [P_n = ∅]` factors out, leaving `∏_{p|W} p/(p-1) = W/φ(W)` on the coprime branch. **Empirical verification**: 10 conductors `W ∈ {2, 6, 30, 210, 2310 (primorials); 15, 105 (squarefree non-primorial); 12, 60, 420 (non-squarefree, rad ∈ {6, 30, 210})}` × `N ∈ {10⁵, 10⁶}`. F1/F2/F3 (pointwise identity at squarefree primorial / squarefree non-primorial / radical-reduced non-squarefree): max abs_err 8.88·10⁻¹⁶ (machine epsilon). F4 (mean = 1): all in [0.999997, 1.000000]. F5 (L² closed form `||T_W^{div} − chi_P||²/N` matches explicit prime-distribution count `(n_coprime_primes (T_val−1)² + n_coprime_comp T_val² + n_W) / N` with `T_val = (π(N)/N) · W/φ(W)`): rel_err < 10⁻⁹ on every cell. F6 (Pearson_full > Pearson_div for W ∈ {6, 30, 210, 2310}): PASS, gap = 0.028, 0.089, 0.177, 0.281 respectively at N=10⁵; gap = 0 at W=2 because the only squarefree integers ≤ 2 are exactly the divisors of 2. **Algorithmic consequence**: T_W^{div}(n) is informationally equivalent to a wheel-rad(W) sieve scaled by π(N)/N · rad(W)/φ(rad(W)); the divisor-only restriction inherits all sieve barriers (no polylog opening). The Pearson gap to full T_W locates the prime-discriminating signal in the **non-divisor squarefree conductors** q ≤ W (q squarefree but q ∤ W), capturing 31% of the full Pearson at W=2310. **What's new beyond S191 (C9)**: S191 stated only the corollary at coprime n (`T_W | gcd=1 = (π/N)·W/φ(W)`); S208 proves the identity **on the full domain** (zero at non-coprime n) for all squarefree W, with explicit radical reduction for non-squarefree W. **Refines E2.1**: the wheel-density factor φ(W)/W is now the value of an explicit pointwise scalar field, not merely an asymptotic rank quotient. **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE; project-internal one-line Möbius collapse. **Falsifier**: any (W, n) with `M_W^{div}(n) ≠ [gcd(n, rad(W)) = 1] · rad(W)/φ(rad(W))` by more than 10⁻¹², OR mean ⟨M_W^{div}⟩_n ≠ 1 ± O(1/N), OR Pearson(chi_P, T_W) ≤ Pearson(chi_P, T_W^{div}) at any W ≥ 6, OR L² closed form deviates by more than rel_err 10⁻⁹. None observed across 10 W × 2 N cells. **Successors proposed (S208)**: C9.a.i (off-divisor squarefree expansion: decompose 0.281 Pearson gap at W=2310 by conductor q; predicted Selberg-Delange `~1/φ(p)²` per prime contribution, 1 session); C9.a.ii (Lean formalisation of the divisor-only identity via L6 Hölder reduction + Möbius collapse, ~50-line Lean target, 1 session); C9.a.iii (Λ-modulated divisor-only sum `μ²(q)λ(q)c_q(n)/φ(q)` — does the parity-twisted weight also yield a closed-form scalar on the residue lattice mod W?, 1 session). See `experiments/constructions/spike_divisor_only_wheel/`, `archive/sessions/session208_c9a_divisor_only_wheel.md`. | 208 |
    837:| §C9.b.ii NOVELTY_CHALLENGES — Triple correlation T_Q · shift_{h1} · shift_{h2}: does the spike-pointwise framework extend from the S205 two-point shift identity to the three-point shift identity, with closed form matching the truncated Hardy-Littlewood prime triple singular series at primorial W? (cross-domain: NONE; project-internal Ramanujan-sum prime-by-prime factorisation + S208 Möbius collapse + S205 diagonal selection generalised to triple) | BUILT | I | S209 paradigm-shift session (no cross-domain technique imported, per session mode constraint) composing E2.1 + E2.2 + E2.13 + E2.16 + E1.6 + C9 / S191 / S205 / S208. **Theorem (S209, proven analytically; verified empirically to 0.01% at d=20).** For every squarefree primorial integer W ≥ 1 and integers (h_1, h_2), ⟨T_W^{div}(n) T_W^{div}(n+h_1) T_W^{div}(n+h_2)⟩_n = (π(N)/N)^3 · ∏_{p|W} (p − ν_p(0, h_1, h_2)) · p² / (p−1)^3 = (π(N)/N)^3 · S_HL^{(W)}(0, h_1, h_2), where ν_p({0, h_1, h_2}) is the number of distinct residues mod p and S_HL^{(W)} is the conductor-W truncation of the Hardy-Littlewood prime triple singular series. **Proof (direct)**: cube T_W^{div}(n) = (π/N)·(W/φ(W))·[gcd(n, W) = 1]; the triple coprimality indicator factorises over primes | W; (1/p) Σ_{r mod p} (1−[p|r])(1−[p|r+h_1])(1−[p|r+h_2]) = (p − ν_p)/p; multiply by (W/φ(W))^3 = ∏_{p|W} (p/(p-1))^3. **Independent prime-by-prime derivation (Ramanujan-Fourier)**: G_p(h_1, h_2) = 1 + (1/(p-1)²)·[c_p(h_1) + c_p(h_2) + c_p(h_2 - h_1)] − (1/(p-1)³)·f_p(h_1, h_2) with f_p ∈ {(p-1)(p-2), -(p-2), +2} for ν_p ∈ {1, 2, 3} respectively, verified algebraically equal to (p − ν_p)·p²/(p-1)³ across 70/70 prime × shift cells. **Empirical verification**: 5 primorial W ∈ {2, 6, 30, 210, 2310} × 12 admissible (h_1, h_2) triples × 3 N-values d ∈ {16, 18, 20}: ratio R_emp / pred uniformly in [0.99915, 1.00138] (max deviation 0.14% at d=16, ≤0.005% at d=20). Pre-stated 0.5% band exceeded by 100× at d=20. F3 HL recovery at Q ≈ √N (general T_Q, not divisor-restricted): R / [(π/N)^3 S_HL_full(0,h_1,h_2)] within 1% across all 6 admissible (h_1, h_2) tested at d=20. F4 self-consistency at h_1 = h_2 = 0: ratio = 1.00000 ± 1e-5. F5 (h_2 = 0 reduces to S205 pair × W/φ(W)): exact algebraic identity. F6 (prime triple density): 5% deviation from full HL at d=20 (standard finite-N rate). **What's new beyond S205**: S205 covers the 2-point shift case via diagonal q1=q2 contraction; S209 extends to 3-point shift where the diagonal contraction is INSUFFICIENT (off-diagonal cross-pair terms contribute essentially), but the divisor-restricted T_W^{div} factorises cleanly via S208's Möbius collapse, giving exact closed form at primorial W. **What's new beyond S208**: S208 was the 1-point identity (M_W^{div}(n) = [gcd=1]·W/φ(W)); S209 promotes this to the 3-point correlation, exhibiting the wheel-W indicator's 3-point function as exactly the prime-3-tuple HL singular series at conductor W. **What's new beyond E2.13**: E2.13 is the 4-point cube U^2 / HL match; S209 is the 3-point shift identity, a different observable in the Schwinger-Dyson hierarchy not derivable from E2.13. **What's new beyond E2.16**: E2.16 ruled out kernel-DPP/PPP descriptions of primes by noting 3-point HL factors over primes (not pairs); S209 exhibits the explicit pointwise scalar field T_W^{div} whose 3-point function REALISES this prime-factored 3-tuple singular series — turning E2.16's negative shape into a positive identity. **No polylog opening**: cost is O(N · |H|^2) per (h_1, h_2) batch on T_W^{div}; the construction provides a closed-form numerical evaluator of S_HL^{(W)} at O(W · |H|²) cost (faster than any direct prime-triple sieve), but is structural not algorithmic. **Cross-domain ingredient**: NONE — paradigm-shift session mode forbade cross-domain imports. The argument uses only Möbius inclusion-exclusion (S208), Ramanujan sums (S205), and direct prime-by-prime factorisation. **Falsifier**: any primorial W and admissible (h_1, h_2) with R_emp / pred outside [0.99, 1.01] at d ≥ 18 — empirically falsified to <0.01% across 5×12×3 = 180 cells. OR exhibition of polylog evaluation of ⟨T_W^{div}(n) T_W^{div}(n+h_1) T_W^{div}(n+h_2)⟩ at W ≈ √N as primality-triple witness — would resolve polylog π(x). **Successors (S209)**: C9.b.iv (k=4 four-point at primorial W, predicted closed form (π/N)^4 · ∏_{p|W} (p − ν_p) p^3 / (p−1)^4, 1 session); C9.b.v (general-Q off-diagonal calibration: predict the F2 over-count from missing subsets analytically, 1 session); C9.b.vi (Lean formalisation of the 3-point identity via the S208 Möbius collapse + 3-coprimality factorisation, ~30-line Lean target, 1-2 sessions). See `experiments/constructions/spike_pointwise_HL_triple/`, `archive/sessions/session209_c9b_ii_triple_correlation.md`. | 210 |
